        "thinking": "I'm planning the proportions for a low-poly African bush elephant model, working out realistic shoulder height, body length, and head dimensions to establish the basic skeleton before placing coordinates.\n\nNow I'm sketching out the skeleton hierarchy, with a pelvis root connecting through spine segments to the neck and head, then branching into a trunk chain, tusks, and jaw joints to capture elephant-specific anatomy.\n\nFor the ears, I'm weighing options between a smooth extruded membrane with gradient shading (pink inner, veined gray outer) versus a faceted low-poly plate with distinct corners, leaning toward the faceted look to match the overall stylized aesthetic while still rigging it to hinge joints for flexibility.\n\nFor the legs, I'm planning joint chains from shoulder to foot for front legs and hip to foot for hind legs, using three joints each (shoulder-elbow-wrist, hip-knee-ankle) to give the pillar-like elephant legs a slight natural bend.\n\nFor the feet, I'm planning a rounded cylindrical stub beneath each leg's end point, capped flat, with small flattened nail shapes attached near the front.\n\nFor the body, I'm switching to a loft-based approach along a spine chain, defining control points from rump to shoulders to shape the torso.\n\nStill working out cross-sections...\n\nContinuing up the body toward the chest and neck, I'm sizing the head as a skull box positioned where the neck meets the trunk base, estimating dome height and width relative to shoulder height. Now I'm starting to lay out the spine bone chain beginning at the pelvis root.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether to merge the spine and neck into one continuous chain rather than separate lofts, since the neck is short and thick and could just extend the body loft toward the head and skull. Simplifying to a single \"spine\" chain rooted at the pelvis with path points running from pelvis through the body seems cleanest.\n\nNow I'm working out the neck chain's span count an