ning an African-style ear outline with a wide tapering lobe that comes to a point, then extruding it along the outward axis to give it thickness on the correct side depending on left or right.\n\nWorking out the ear's pose now: angling it so it flares outward about 20° from the head rather than sitting flush, mimicking how relaxed ears flap slightly back and sideways against the shoulder.\n\nI'll settle on roughly a 50° swing, defining the ear's extrusion direction so it points outward and back, with a hinge joint at the attachment point oriented along that outward axis for the flap's rotation.\n\nThinking about the hinge further, I decide a two-joint ear chain (attach joint plus a tip joint) makes more sense so the ear can bend partway along its length rather than acting as one rigid flap.\n\nI'm simplifying to a single rigid ear extrude per side with stacked plates: a gray base plate, plus a slightly smaller pink lining plate offset toward the front face to suggest inner ear detail, keeping orientation of the thickness faces in mind for which side gets color.\n\nSince the ear swings back, its front face (+Z) reads as the inner ear facing forward on both sides, so I'll paint using the normal's z-component -- strong positive z gets the pink inner color, strong negative gets outer gray, and the thin side walls get a rim tone. For the inner face I'll add subtle vein detail using cell-border noise patterns to break up the flat color, plus a darker tone along the rim fold for depth.\n\nI'll skip the two-piece ear idea and stick with a single joint per ear for simplicity, keeping the rest pose neutral with limbs extended. Moving on to legs, I'll build these using the limb() function, starting with the front leg's shoulder joint positioned inside the body.\n\nFor the front leg, I'm placing the shoulder, elbow, wrist, and foot targets with specific coordinates, checking that segment lengths roughly sum to the vertical span between shoulder and foot so the leg stays only barely bent, with the elbow hinting slightly backward for a natural stance.\n\nI'm defining a radius sweep along each leg chain tapering from wider at the shoulder to narrower near the ankle, then widening again slightly at the foot cushion for that columnar elephant-leg look.\n\nNow I'm sizing the foot as a short flat-capped cylinder sitting near ground level, slightly wider at the bottom than the top, and adding small cream nail boxes along the front of the foot—four for front feet, three or four for hind.\n\nI'm working out the hind leg joint chain: hip joint angled slightly inward, bending forward at the knee before dropping down to the hock and foot, setting rough segment lengths and positions for that two-segment limb.\n\nI'm also tapering the back leg's radius from a wider thigh bulge at top down to a narrower ankle, and planning to add toenails near the foot's tip.\n\nFor skin texture I'm thinking through a countershade base between darker back and lighter belly tones, then layering subtle cell-like crackle patterns to mimic elephant skin wrinkles without overcomplicating the low-poly look.\n\nAdding dust-gradient shading toward the legs, small amber eyes with wrinkled brow rings and lash spikes, a simple lower-jaw mouth piece, and a chained tail of joints extending from the rump.\n\nFor the tail tip, I'm opting for a tuft of black bristle cards rather than a ring of cone spikes to keep part count manageable while still giving a believable bushy end.\n\nI also want to scatter sparse hair cards across the forehead and back using a thin bristle texture, keeping the count low (~80) with dark tinting for a realistic touch. Then I'm adding wrinkle detail at the leg joints using dark looped sweeps around the knees and elbows to suggest skin creases.\n\nI should add pink depigmented mottling on ears, trunk, and face where noise exceeds a threshold, plus inner ear pink coloring with veins, keeping colors flat and under the 64-color limit. For footprint placement I'm adjusting the foot cylinders to sink slightly into the floor for contact realism while keeping the trunk tip elevated above ground.\n\nNow I want to verify the lowest points of the tusks and tail tuft also sit near the floor appropriately, and before writing the body loft I should check how the builder's loft options are typed—specifically what fields like bone, color, and sides are expected—so I'll search the source for the loft section definitions.\n\n",
